CORRECTION (2026-07-30): what this file said about unifont was wrong
---------------------------------------------------------------------

It said:

    "Its pack ships only a two-line README (name + URL) and its TTF carries NO licence
     record at all, so there is nothing on disk to extract."

The second half is false. unifont.ttf carries its grant in `name` table record **0**
(Copyright), verbatim:

    Copyright (C) 2014 Roman Czyborra, Paul Hardy, Qianqian Fang, Andrew Miller, et al.
    Licensed under the GNU General Public License; either version 2, or (at your option) a
    later version, with the GNU Font Embedding Exception.

The earlier check looked at record **13** (License Description) — which is where DejaVu's
notice lives, because that is the record the DejaVu work had just been resolved from — found
record 13 absent, and concluded the font carried nothing. A font may put its terms in either
record, and many put them in both. **Check every name record before concluding a binary is
silent.** The cost of that mistake here was recording a release blocker as "needs an external
source" when the answer was already in the tree — and the exception text it said was missing
turned out to be on disk too, in full, inside xolonium-regular.otf.


Closing nimbussansl: what it took, and the one thing not achieved
-------------------------------------------------------------------

The plan was to byte-match our n019004l.pfb against a package and adopt that package's licence.
**The byte match was not obtained**, and LICENSE.nimbussansl says so plainly rather than
implying a stronger result. What the download established instead:

  ours                       pfaedit,            2002-12-31   72,400 bytes   Notice: 2001-2002
  gsfonts 8.14+v8.11+urw     pfaedit,            2005-03-27   73,988 bytes   Notice: 2001-2002
  gsfonts urwcyr1.0.7~pre44  FontForge 20070723, 2007-08-02   70,730 bytes   Notice: 2001-2005

Three regenerations of the SAME work at different dates -- identical /FontName, /FullName and
/version, same two rights holders -- and ours is the oldest, predating Debian's surviving
archive (earliest gsfonts upload 2005). The 2005 copy's /Notice string is byte-identical to
ours. So the licence is established by provenance rather than byte-identity, and that
distinction is recorded rather than glossed.

The lesson generalises: a byte match is the strongest evidence, but its ABSENCE is not evidence
against. Chasing the exact build is worth one bounded attempt; after that, converging metadata
from independent sources is a legitimate answer as long as the weaker basis is written down.

Rule for anything added here
------------------------------

Obtain licence text from the work itself or from the font project, and add it as
LICENSE.<name>. Do not paraphrase or reconstruct from memory, and do not accept a SUMMARY of a
licence page as the text — these are legal documents and only the authoritative copy is any
use. State in the file where the copy came from, so the next reader can judge it.

One more trap, found while closing unifont: a project's CURRENT licence page may describe a
LATER release than the binary in this tree. unifoundry.com today states SIL OFL v1.1 for
compiled fonts; the unifont.ttf here is 7.0.06 from 2014 and declares GPLv2+ in its own name
table. A binary is governed by the terms it shipped under, not by terms its upstream adopted
afterwards. Re-derive from the binary on every font upgrade.


Nothing here blocks development. The one remaining item (nimbussansl's grant) wants closing
before a public release that ships these fonts.

